I see it as rallying, and encouraging people to view other
possibilities.  I don't think that's nothing.

I don't pretend to be a software project manager, and I don't know
nethack nearly as well as many other people - I've never even
ascended.  You're asking good questions, but there's the danger that
any new effort is dead in the cradle if any one person is required to
come up with all the answers to get it off the ground.  Nethack is too
complex.  I'd like to see a communal effort from the start, with a
core of people taking the lead.

Before you bristle, know that I'm not volunteering any other
individual's time.  But I am promoting an alternative which I see as
more effective and productive than the broken system now in place.
People are already expending energy coding and bugfixing.  With the
right framework, that effort could be put to better use.

